      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's dive straight into the technical details:\n\n1. **Verifying OOMKilled vs. Process Exiting**: To determine if your auth-service pod is being OOMKilled (Out of Memory Killed), you can check the pod's events for any OOMKilled messages. Run the following command to get a list of all events for your pod:\n\n   ```\n   kubectl describe pod default/auth-service-7f8d\n   ```\n\n   Look for any events that mention \"OOMKilled\". If you find them, it means your pod was killed due to exceeding its memory limits. If the events don't mention OOMKilled, it's likely that the process exited due to some other reason, such as a crash or a misconfiguration.\n\n2. **Checking Liveness/Readiness Probes**: To see if the liveness or readiness probes are causing the restarts, you can use the following commands:\n\n   - For liveness probes:\n     ```\n     kubectl describe pod default/auth-service-7f8d | grep -i liveness\n     ```\n\n   - For readiness probes:\n     ```\n     kubectl describe pod default/auth-service-7f8d | grep -i readiness",
